Go splits failure into two kinds, and the choice depends on who can fix it. If the caller could do something about it — retry, show a message, fall back — return an error value. A file that doesn't exist, a request with bad input, a database that's down: those are expected failures, so (result, error) is the contract.

A panic says something is wrong with the program itself. An index that should always be valid because you proved the math, a map that was initialized in the same function, a switch that handled every case — if those fail, no caller can fix it; the code is wrong. That's what panic is for.

The practical line I use: panic when the bug would be silently corrupting if continued; error when the failure is a legitimate outcome of the request. A library that panics on user input pushes its bugs into every caller's crash log — which is why well-behaved packages convert even their internal panics to errors at the package boundary.

```go
// error: caller can handle — return it
if err := save(); err != nil {
    return fmt.Errorf("save: %w", err)
}
// panic: invariant broken — the program is wrong
index := computeIndex()  // PROVEN safe by construction
_ = items[index]         // if this panics, the proof was wrong
```

When panic is called, the current function stops at that line. Its deferred calls run — this is the key behavior — then the caller's deferred calls, and so on up the goroutine's stack, in reverse order of how they were set up. That's LIFO: what was deferred last runs first.

If any deferred function along the way calls recover, the unwinding stops there and that function returns normally. If none does, the whole program prints the panic value with a goroutine dump and exits with a non-zero status. One goroutine's unrecovered panic kills every goroutine — the process goes down together.

```go
func safeCall() (err error) {
    defer func() {
        if r := recover(); r != nil {
            err = fmt.Errorf("panicked: %v", r)  // this defer runs during unwind
        }
    }()
    doWork()  // panics → deferred closure runs LIFO → converted to err
    return nil
}
```

Recover is the hatch in the middle of a panic. It only does something when called inside a deferred function during an active panic on the same goroutine: it stops the unwind, the deferred function returns normally, and the panic value is handed to you as the return of recover.

Called anywhere else — outside a defer, after the unwind finished, on a different goroutine — recover returns nil and does nothing. There is no 'check for panics' you can do proactively; you can only be the deferred function that catches one in flight.

The idiomatic use is exactly one: a package boundary. A library function that might panic internally (because of a bug or an intentional invariant check) wraps its body in a deferred recover so its callers see an error value instead of a crash. Anything more elaborate — recover as control flow — is fighting the language.

```go
func runSafely(fn func()) {
    defer func() {
        if r := recover(); r != nil {
            log.Printf("worker panic: %v", r)  // recover ONLY inside defer
        }
    }()
    fn()
}
// recover() outside a defer returns nil — never catches anything
```

Why the defer-only restriction? Because recover works by intercepting the unwind, and the unwind only runs through deferred calls. There's no stack inspection at arbitrary points, no exception object you can query — the mechanism is literally: the runtime is walking out of your function, defer gave you a callback on the way out, and recover says 'stop here, hand me the reason.'

The goroutine restriction matters in practice: a deferred recover in goroutine A cannot catch a panic in goroutine B. Each goroutine unwinds its own stack, which means code that spawns goroutines must give each one its own recover at its own boundary — usually a small wrapper function:

```go
func runSafely(fn func()) {
    defer func() { if r := recover(); r != nil { log.Printf("worker panic: %v", r) } }()
    fn()
}
```

Without that, any panic in a worker goroutine takes down the whole server, no matter how many recovers the parent function has.

It does, and this surprises people who expect exception-like isolation. Go's runtime does not try to unwind other goroutines or isolate the failure — the process exits. Every goroutine, including the main one and every in-flight request, dies immediately.

The consequence is a rule for real services: any goroutine you spawn gets its own deferred recover at its own boundary. The parent's recover doesn't cover the child — they're separate stacks. If a server accepts a connection and handles it in a goroutine, that handler needs a top-level recover that converts panics into a 500 (or at least a logged crash of that one request) rather than taking down the whole server with it.

```go
// every spawned goroutine needs its own boundary
go func() {
    defer func() {
        if r := recover(); r != nil {
            log.Printf("worker panic: %v", r)  // this request dies; server lives
        }
    }()
    handle(conn)
}()
```

It looks like try/catch if you squint, but it isn't. Exceptions are a control-flow primitive: any frame can throw, any ancestor catches, and the compiler weaves the propagation through every call. Go's error path is none of that — an error is a return value, visible in the signature, checked at the call site with `if err != nil`. The propagation is the explicit return chain, not the runtime's unwinding.

Panic/recover exists for the other kind of failure: the program is wrong. It's a crash mechanism — with a hatch so boundaries can convert the crash into a value. That's why the idioms never overlap: you don't catch panics the way you catch exceptions, and you don't use errors for impossible states.

The tell that they're different: in exception languages, adding a throw to a function changes nothing in the signature — callers find out at runtime. In Go, a function that can fail says so in its return type, and the compiler makes you deal with the error before using the result. The visibility is the design, and panic/recover deliberately doesn't have it.

```go
// errors: the visible path
if err != nil { return err }

// recover: only inside defer, only at boundaries
defer func() {
    if r := recover(); r != nil {
        err = fmt.Errorf("panicked: %v", r)  // crash → error, at ONE seam
    }
}()
```
